### Tested versions

Reproducible in v4.8.dev.custom_build [46173009d]

### System information

Linux (pop_os 22.04 LTS) AMD 5950

### Issue description

If you write a gdscript like:

```gdscript
class_name Blah
extends TestNativeClass

func _init() -> void:
    super(1)
```

And a native class like:
```C++
class TestNativeClass : public RefCounted {
	GDCLASS(TestNativeClass, RefCounted)

private:
	int value;

protected:
	static void _bind_methods()
	{
		ClassDB::bind_method(D_METHOD("_init", "value"), &TestNativeClass::_init);
	}

public:
	void _init(int p_value) { value = p_value; }

};
```

The super(1) call will be silently discarded. The GDScript compiler will know about the arguments and it will otherwise act like it should work. There is a check to explicitly discard calls to native _init in `OPCODE_CALL_SELF_BASE` in `gdscript_vm.cpp`

```C++
				if (E) {
					*dst = E->value->call(p_instance, (const Variant **)argptrs, argc, err);
				} else if (gds->native.ptr()) {
					if (*methodname != GDScriptLanguage::get_singleton()->strings._init) {
						const MethodBind *mb = ClassDB::get_method(gds->native->get_name(), *methodname);
						if (!mb) {
							err.error = Callable::CallError::CALL_ERROR_INVALID_METHOD;
						} else {
							*dst = mb->call(p_instance->owner, (const Variant **)argptrs, argc, err);
						}
					} else {
						err.error = Callable::CallError::CALL_OK;
					}
				} else {
					if (*methodname != GDScriptLanguage::get_singleton()->strings._init) { ** EXPLICIT CHECK HERE **
						err.error = Callable::CallError::CALL_ERROR_INVALID_METHOD;
					} else {
						err.error = Callable::CallError::CALL_OK;
					}
				}
```

So this is clearly some sort of intentional check, but this check is adding overhead to every native call, and silently breaking convention here. This design decision goes back very far, back to the first github commit.

I don't know what the fix should be (I would prefer removing that _init silent fail check entirely). If there is another way to achieve this functionality (to have mandatory initialization) in native extensions then maybe it should be indicated somewhere, e.g. during script compile or something.

### Steps to reproduce

I created a repo here: 

https://github.com/not-magic/GDExtension-native-super-bug

If you build/open the main.tscn you can see how the super(1,2) call is not respected

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

See the repro in steps above for link to a project

### Tested versions

- Reproducible in v4.7.2.stable.mono.official [ed1daf0bf]

### System information

Godot v4.7.2.stable.mono - Linux Mint 22 (Wilma) on X11 - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 2 monitors - Vulkan (Mobile) - dedicated NVIDIA GeForce GTX 1650 with Max-Q Design - 11th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i5-11400H @ 2.70GHz (12 threads) - 15.32 GiB memory - PulseAudio (44100 Hz, Stereo/mono)

### Issue description

I was attempting to retrieve the NavigationRegion3D of a point on a path, as suggested in the [documentation](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/navigation/navigation_using_navigationpathqueryobjects.html#path-metadata).

While this presumably works fine in GDScript, I noticed that GodotObject.InstanceFromId() expects the ID to be a `ulong`, while NavigationPathQueryResult3D.PathOwnerIds returns an array of `long`s. 

This inconsistency means you need to cast the OwnerId from the path to a ulong, although it  seems to have worked in my testing so far, seems to not be intended and I'm not sure if it'll always behave correctly.

### Steps to reproduce

```cs
using Godot;

// This example won't run, but that is fine, the bug is a compile-time problem
public partial class TestNode : Node {
    NavigationPathQueryResult3D res = new NavigationPathQueryResult3D();
    public override void _Ready() {

        // This doesn't work (Argument 1: cannot convert from 'long' to 'ulong')
        NavigationRegion3D region = (NavigationRegion3D) GodotObject.InstanceFromId(res.PathOwnerIds[0]);

        // This does work, and from my testing seems to return the correct object
        NavigationRegion3D region2 = (NavigationRegion3D) GodotObject.InstanceFromId((ulong) res.PathOwnerIds[0]);
    }

}
```

